# The pokeplatinum pc/ engine commit this client's fused build is known to
# build against. The client fuses with the engine as a mod (mmo/mods/openmmo),
# so it is authored against engine sources that advance in the engine repo next
# door. `make -C mmo enginecheck` compares $ENGINE_DIR's HEAD to the commit
# below and warns loudly on drift; `make -C mmo fused` runs that check first, so
# a fused build that breaks after an engine bump reports "the engine moved"
# instead of a confusing compile error deep in engine sources.
#
# This sha is only half the contract. The other half is mmo/ENGINE_SURFACE: the
# engine paths this build depends on by content, each with the hash it had when
# this commit was verified. A sha alone cannot say whether an engine that moved
# moved anywhere that matters, the 2026-08-15 bump crossed 43 commits and
# 6,080 changed files, of which 3 touched anything here; this bump crosses 146
# commits and adds the runtime content door (pc_modfs, the narc hook,
# MapHeader_Lookup, extra-prop).
#
# To bump, and the order matters:
#   1. `make -C mmo pincheck` and read the diff of every path it names.
#   2. `make -C mmo patchcheck`, every hunk still on exact context.
#   3. `make -C mmo fused` builds, and the five fused checks in `make test`
#      boot it: crowd, heap, name, label, ceiling.
#   4. `make -C mmo abicheck`.
#   5. Replace the sha below, then `mmo/tools/engine_surface.sh --update`, and
#      commit the two together. pincheck fails on a sha whose manifest was
#      written for a different one, so half a bump cannot pass unnoticed.
# Why the pin moves at all, and how rarely: mmo/mods/openmmo/README.md,
# "Pinning the engine, and knowing when it moves".
#
# The guard compares committed HEAD only; the engine's uncommitted working-tree
# changes are not captured here.
#
# Verified 2026-09-03: pincheck, patchcheck every hunk on exact context,
# fused builds and links, the five fused checks (crowd, heap, name, label,
# ceiling) green, abicheck green. This bump crosses TWO commits, both in
# the rasterizer. First the perspective divide: posX * viewport width was
# a 32-bit product, which the console never overflows (256 pixels, 24-bit
# W) and a wide HD viewport of up to 2736 pixels does for any vertex far
# enough away, sending it to the far side of the screen, the smear every
# wide HD run has carried, the width cliffs pc_view.c was clamped against,
# and the black notch from the same wrap in Y. The product is 64-bit now,
# which is bit-identical at native, and the RAM cap counts what it drops.
# Second the screen x field: it was sized from 512 * hd alone, so a page
# wider than 512 columns folded at 1023 pixels (hd3d 2) or 2047 (hd3d 4);
# it is sized from the page's own width now, the console's at native.
